  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The quantity of fat, in grams per kilogram, in a sausage\n- The number of points out of $50$ that a student gets in a math test\n- The weight, measured  in kilograms, of a randomly selected seventeen-year-old student",
  "worked_solution": "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095039/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,1\\,000].$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\u2026,50,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III describes a  continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
